  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Nikita mentally runs through absurd ways to steal Nagiyev's keys—from sleeping pills to faking a heart attack—before grabbing them while Nagiyev steps away, only to get caught immediately.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где я сейчас снотворное возьму?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда пробуем вариант номер два.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Помогите, нужно в скорую звонить!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "У меня же телефон вон лежит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда у меня варианты закончились.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну ладно, сегодня победил лосось.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем он мне это сказал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Никита, ты зачем ключи от машины взял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Ну что, пока не получилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, пока ничего не вышло.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Врёшь ты лучше, чем тачки угоняешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, я обязательно достану ключи.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где я сейчас снотворное возьму?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where am I going to get sleeping pills right now?' Nikita raises the pitch on 'will take' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда пробуем вариант номер два.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then let's try option number two.' The grammar packs 'then' and 'two' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Помогите, нужно в скорую звонить!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Help, we need to call an ambulance!' In Russian, the idea of 'help' flows naturally into 'to call' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "У меня же телефон вон лежит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My phone is lying right over there.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'at' and finishing on 'is lying'. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда у меня варианты закончились.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then I have run out of options.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'then' and finishing on 'are over'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну ладно, сегодня победил лосось.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright then, today the salmon won.' The grammar packs 'come on' and 'salmon' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем он мне это сказал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why did he tell me that?' Nikita anchors the line on 'why', while 'said' completes the thought. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Никита, ты зачем ключи от машины взял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nikita, why did you take the car keys?' Dmitry Nagiyev addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'took'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Ну что, пока не получилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, did it not work out yet?' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'turned out' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, пока ничего не вышло.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, nothing worked out yet.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'turned out' to negate the statement. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Врёшь ты лучше, чем тачки угоняешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You lie much better than you steal cars.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'are lying' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, я обязательно достану ключи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, I will definitely get the keys.' In Russian, the idea of 'wait' flows naturally into 'the keys' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
